Not to say Steve Warren is wrong, but when he says 

>But he was always against the darker forces.  If he was chaotic he would have
>been aliagned with them.  Instead, whenever he came up against the dark
>spirits and sorcerers he destroyed them (with a shiver).

I have to disagree.  According the the Player's Handbook in AD&D, a character
can be any of nine different alignments, ranging from Lawful Good to
Chaotic Evil.  Lawful means that the person believes in authority, order, and
government, etc.  while chaotic means that a person believes in himself
over society.  This is not necessarily an evil person.  A temple of devil
worshippers is Lawful Evil, believing in order, but also being evil.  
Following this trend, Conan would be Chaotic Good, believing in himself and
not any particular government, but also hating evil.

This most likely would mean that Crom was chaotic also, believing in the 
individual, not in a ruling society.

I just had to get my two cents worth in.

